    {
      "id": 1,
      "text": "Medusa was first settled in 1783 by Uriah Hall and his son Joshua, who built the first grist mill on Ten Mile Creek.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        9,
        23
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia",
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Medusa%2C_New_York",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The hamlet was originally called Hall's Mills, renamed Medusa in 1850 because another community had the same name, forcing a new post office.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1,
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/medusa_new_york",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "In the 19th century Medusa boasted a Methodist Episcopal Church, a Christian Church, a hotel, two stores, and two grain mills that turned grain into flour.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        12,
        27,
        28
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia",
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Medusa%2C_New_York",
      "category": "culture",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "A paper mill powered by a 1899 dam and a cheese factory were notable industrial ventures that marked Medusa’s economic diversity.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/medusa_new_york",
      "category": "economy",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The Medusa Fire of May 25, 1932, destroyed several buildings, reshaping the hamlet’s landscape.",
